## Backups and restore

The cron Worker dumps every table to a bucket of its own (`{{projectName}}-backups`, which no other Worker binds) each day (`backups/<date>/<time>/<table>/0001.ndjson` and a `manifest.json`), keeps 30 days of backups and runs one on demand when `POST /backup/run` carries `BACKUP_TRIGGER_TOKEN` as a bearer token, the value you stored at the first deploy. The media are not copied: they are already in the media bucket, and the `media` table of the dump is their index. D1 Time Travel rewinds the whole database to a point of the last 30 days and covers nothing of R2.

To restore, rewind the database with `wrangler d1 time-travel restore DB --timestamp=<ISO 8601 time>` (from `apps/web`), or bring back the rows of one table from a backup: download its objects with `wrangler r2 object get {{projectName}}-backups/backups/<date>/<time>/<table>/0001.ndjson --remote --file <file>`. Each line is one row, a JSON object of column names and values: write the rows as `INSERT OR REPLACE` statements and run them with `wrangler d1 execute DB --remote --file <file.sql>`. The files of the media are still in the bucket.

